WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.860
SpaceX had a great 2020.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:06.660
They delivered astronauts to the ISS, began
rolling out their satellite internet service,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.440
and successfully flew a Starship prototype.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.430
But just one year earlier, things weren’t
looking so good.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:18.540
The number of SpaceX launches had dropped
from 21 to just 13 and the problem was obvious:

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.440
there just weren’t enough customers.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.940
Even though Elon had delivered on his promise
of affordable and reusable rockets, commercial

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.820
satellite companies weren’t moving fast
enough to keep up.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:31.910
None of that mattered though, because Elon
is great at thinking ahead and had already

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.429
set a plan in motion to fill the slack capacity.

00:00:34.429 --> 00:00:39.079
Obviously, I’m talking about Starlink here,
but that’s not the only way that SpaceX

00:00:39.079 --> 00:00:43.679
is making money, they actually have 3 distinct
sources of revenue with completely unique

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:44.949
business models, so let’s dive in and figure
out exactly how much each business makes.

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:50.460
The first SpaceX business is extremely straightforward:
people pay SpaceX to launch stuff into space

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:51.460
for them.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.920
Sometimes they are launching satellites for
private companies, other times they are delivering

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.120
supplies to the ISS for NASA.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.179
But conceptually, the model is simple: they
build a rocket and then someone pays them

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:04.180
to send it into space with their payload on
top.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:08.790
Now, these launches get a ton of attention,
after all, they are extremely fun to watch.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:14.060
But the market for rocket launches is actually
pretty small at only $8 billion dollars globally,

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.580
and I can think of a few countries that definitely
won’t be buying SpaceX rockets anytime soon.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:23.360
So SpaceX will basically never be able to
capture the entire global launch market, but

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.940
the company is valued at $74 billion dollars,
so something interesting must be going on.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.150
Well, there are a few important factors that
are unique to the space industry.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.520
First, it’s growing incredibly fast.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.860
Even though the market is only $8 billion
right now, analysts estimate that the market

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.860
will be nearly 5 times that in 2030.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.030
There aren’t really any other industries
growing that quickly and SpaceX is in the

00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:50.580
perfect position to ride that growth to market
dominance.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.670
Honestly, SpaceX already has a dominant position
as it is.

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.770
In 2018, they pulled in an estimated $2 billion
dollars in revenue.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.270
Which was more than all of their direct competitors,
although the United Launch Authority was pretty

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.270
close that year.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:09.159
The dynamics of the launch market are changing
quickly, and this is what’s driving SpaceX’s

00:02:09.159 --> 00:02:10.319
continued growth.

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:13.579
There are four key categories to consider
when looking at rocket launches: small, medium,

00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:14.579
heavy, and super heavy.

00:02:14.579 --> 00:02:15.612
Small covers anything under 1.5 metric tons,
medium is under 8.5 tons, heavy is under 30

00:02:15.612 --> 00:02:16.612
tons and anything over 30 tons is considered
super heavy.

00:02:16.612 --> 00:02:20.920
In 2020, the market for super heavy launches
was zero because the capability just didn’t

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:21.920
exist yet.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.890
But SpaceX’s new Big Falcon Rocket and Starship
will finally make this a possibility.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:28.200
And this is a huge opportunity.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.820
The super heavy launch market is expected
to be over $13 billion dollars by 2030, which

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.879
is almost twice what the entire launch market
is today, and SpaceX will clearly be the major

00:02:37.879 --> 00:02:39.319
player in that category.

00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:43.870
See, when it comes to valuing technology companies,
market size is incredibly important.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.430
The car market is $80 billion dollars in the
US alone, and is in the trillions globally.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:52.879
That’s absolutely massive and a huge reason
why Tesla is so valuable.

00:02:52.879 --> 00:02:56.189
If everyone starts buying Teslas, the company
is going to make a fortune.

00:02:56.189 --> 00:02:57.989
There’s a twist here though.

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:02.409
Although the car market is absolutely massive,
it isn’t really growing.

00:03:02.409 --> 00:03:06.560
In fact, in some parts of the world, people
are buying less cars on average than they

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:07.560
used to.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.959
This makes sense though because technology
has made it easier to get by without a car.

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:15.609
Apps like Uber and Lyft made ridesharing a
viable alternative to owning a car in many

00:03:15.609 --> 00:03:19.239
situations, especially in crowded cities with
expensive parking.

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:23.920
And there is still a huge unanswered question
as to how self-driving cars will affect purchasing

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:24.920
behavior.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.139
It’s clearly not going to happen this year,
but it’s totally possible that self-driving

00:03:28.139 --> 00:03:34.050
cars will eventually be available, on demand,
at such a scale that owning a car makes less

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.050
and less sense.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:38.769
So, while the car market might actually shrink
over the next few decades, the opposite is

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:40.019
true for space.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:44.709
Last week, I detailed out Varda is going to
start manufacturing high value items like

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:49.819
human organs and fiber optic cable in space
and if they are successful, Varda is going

00:03:49.819 --> 00:03:52.230
to need a lot of rockets to scale that business.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:56.629
Right now, SpaceX’s customers are mostly
commercial satellite companies and government

00:03:56.629 --> 00:03:57.629
research organizations.

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:01.629
They’ve provided enough business for SpaceX
to make a few billion in revenue from rocket

00:04:01.629 --> 00:04:06.359
launches, but if an entirely new industry
starts taking advantage of space in a completely

00:04:06.359 --> 00:04:10.049
different way, the demand for launches could
explode.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:14.409
But while betting on an entirely new industry
like space manufacturing is exciting.

00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:17.900
Elon has a massive company to run and doesn’t
have time to waste.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.650
We all know how aggressive he is on timelines
after all.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.490
“When I cite a schedule, it is actually
the schedule I think is true.”

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:28.800
Current estimates put SpaceX’s launch business
at around $3 billion dollars.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.449
It’s growing steadily, and is certainly
an impressive accomplishment.

00:04:32.449 --> 00:04:38.030
But now, let’s look at the revenue projections
for SpaceX’s second line of business: StarLink.

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.629
These are some pretty insane growth targets
and I’d totally understand if you thought

00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:46.850
this data was just a joke that Elon tweeted,
but it’s actually from Morgan Stanley analysts.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:51.320
So let’s talk through how Starlink makes
money and why SpaceX is expecting it to be

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:53.070
such a huge area of growth.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:57.740
The core business model for Starlink is quite
simple, even if the technology is extremely

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:58.770
complex.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:03.110
SpaceX basically just charges customers a
monthly subscription fee like any other internet

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:04.259
service provider.

00:05:04.259 --> 00:05:09.169
Currently, customers do have to pay a $500
one-time fee for the equipment, but after

00:05:09.169 --> 00:05:12.210
they’re live, service is just $99 per month.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.800
Early reviews have been pretty good too, with
plenty of customers reporting reliable speeds

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.340
in rural parts of the country where they had
no chance of getting internet service before.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:27.319
As of August 2021, Starlink has 90 thousand
subscribers, and that number is growing over

00:05:27.319 --> 00:05:28.979
20% per month.

00:05:28.979 --> 00:05:30.949
That growth figure is extremely promising.

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:35.830
20% monthly growth is enough to 10x the business
every year if you can keep it up, and Starlink

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:38.020
is showing no signs of slowing down.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:42.720
They already have 700 thousand people signed
up on a waitlist in the US alone.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.039
If SpaceX can convert all of those people
to paying customers, they will be just shy

00:05:47.039 --> 00:05:49.400
of a billion dollars in revenue.

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.650
Pretty incredible stuff.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:55.199
Part of what’s holding back the rollout
currently is that SpaceX needs to launch more

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:57.689
Starlink satellites and fast.

00:05:57.689 --> 00:06:01.830
They currently have a little under 2 thousand
satellites in orbit, but they have approval

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:07.159
to launch nearly 12 thousand satellites, the
only issue is actually getting them up there.

00:06:07.159 --> 00:06:08.830
That’s where Starship comes in.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:13.020
Part of why Elon Musk has been so successful
throughout his career is that he thinks in

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.530
decades, not years.

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:18.610
He’s always looking for ways to build synergies
between the different projects he’s working

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:19.610
on.

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:23.639
The first great example of this was his “secret
plan” for Tesla, which he published on the

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:26.069
Tesla blog back in 2006.

00:06:26.069 --> 00:06:30.970
In the post, Elon outlined how he planned
to take on the entire car market by first

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:35.139
selling an expensive roadster that would compete
with Porsche and Ferrari.

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:40.039
Then use the money from the roadster initiative
to build a sporty four door family car at

00:06:40.039 --> 00:06:41.250
half the price.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.639
That became the Model S that launched in 2012.

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:48.569
Finally, Elon said that once the Model S was
selling well, he would introduce a third model

00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:50.449
that would be even more affordable.

00:06:50.449 --> 00:06:53.949
Clearly he was talking about the Model 3,
which hit roads in 2017.

00:06:53.949 --> 00:06:57.129
Elon is doing something similar at SpaceX
right now.

00:06:57.129 --> 00:07:02.370
His two top priorities are Starship and Starlink
and they have been described as “two Manhattan

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.349
projects running in parallel”.

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:09.130
The scale of the engineering effort is massive,
but because they feed off of each other, they

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:10.909
are more likely to be successful.

00:07:10.909 --> 00:07:15.050
See, Starlink would be extremely costly to
roll out using only Falcon 9 rockets.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:20.139
This is because a Falcon 9 can only fit 60
starlink satellites on board during a single

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:21.139
mission.

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:25.159
At this rate, it would take 200 launches to
get the full Starlink network up and running,

00:07:25.159 --> 00:07:28.940
but Starship can take 400 satellites on a
single trip.

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:33.879
With just 30 launches, SpaceX could have a
massive network of 12,000 Starlink satellites

00:07:33.879 --> 00:07:37.599
in orbit, and they’re launching about 30
rockets per year already.

00:07:37.599 --> 00:07:42.919
So Starlink clearly benefits from Starship’s
incredible payload capacity, but that doesn’t

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:46.830
explain how Starship benefits from Starlink's
satellite internet.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:51.430
The answer comes back to that original problem
Elon ran into back in 2019.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:55.259
The lack of demand for rocket launches was
really hurting SpaceX’s ability to plan

00:07:55.259 --> 00:07:58.020
long term and that was unacceptable.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:02.000
Every SpaceX rocket takes years of research
and development to bring to reality.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:06.470
And no one, not even Elon Musk, can justify
spending billions of dollars and years of

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:09.190
time building rockets that no one is going
to use.

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:11.749
But Starlink solves the demand side of the
equation.

00:08:11.749 --> 00:08:16.340
Now, whenever SpaceX has a rocket ready to
go, but no one to purchase it, they can just

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.590
launch more Starlink satellites.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:23.310
And the revenues from Starlink will allow
Elon to pour more money into future rocket

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:24.310
development.

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:25.310
It doesn’t stop there though.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:29.069
Investors clearly understand how massive the
Starlink opportunity is.

00:08:29.069 --> 00:08:32.300
Remember how the market for launching rockets
is only $8 billion dollars?

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:37.050
Well the market for global broadband internet
is $1 trillion dollars, that’s trillion,

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.050
with a T.

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:41.300
Now, obviously SpaceX isn’t going to capture
that entire market.

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:46.290
People will still be using terrestrial networks
in most situations, especially in cities.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:50.820
But it’s not unreasonable to think that
Starlink could capture 3% of the market and

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:53.889
wind up generating $30 billion per year in
revenue.

00:08:53.889 --> 00:08:58.910
That’s an irresistible opportunity for investors,
and it’s allowed SpaceX to raise billions

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:01.090
of dollars to fund additional expansion.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:06.260
Currently, I’d estimate that SpaceX is making
about $1 million dollars a year from Starlink,

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:10.350
which is a rounding error compared to their
multi-billion dollar launch business, but

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.269
remember: Big things have small beginnings.

00:09:13.269 --> 00:09:17.700
Lots of people credit Elon’s success to
his engineering knowledge, and that’s certainly

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.210
important, but I think it’s his ability
to think long term that’s actually the main

00:09:22.210 --> 00:09:23.950
driver of his success.

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:28.320
He doesn’t mind being doubted or mocked,
in fact, he clearly thrives on it.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:34.010
And no project has been the subject of more
skepticism than point-to-point, which is SpaceX’s

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:36.810
third line of business, although it’s not
live yet.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.910
The promise of SpaceX point-to-point travel
is pretty incredible.

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:44.990
Elon wants to get you from New York to Tokyo
in under 30 minutes, and I totally understand

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:49.240
why people find this so far-fetched, but let’s
walk through the potential problems and then

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:53.850
figure out exactly how much money SpaceX could
make from this business if it works.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.670
The first issue that comes up whenever people
discuss traveling around the world on a rocket

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:58.670
is always safety.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:02.850
I’ve seen plenty of comments on my previous
videos about how astronauts go through years

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:07.920
of training and have to be in peak physical
condition in order to undergo space travel.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:12.019
Astronauts are clearly incredible people,
so no shade to them, but I don’t think the

00:10:12.019 --> 00:10:14.959
actual liftoff is the hardest part of their
job.

00:10:14.959 --> 00:10:20.060
Blue Origin was able to take an 18 year old
and an 82 year old on the same trip to space

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.310
without any issues.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:25.180
Sure, you might need to do some training or
get a certification in order to safely ride

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:30.510
on a SpaceX rocket, but people already jump
through plenty of red tape just to get TSA

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:34.410
precheck or Global Entry, so I don’t think
it’s going to be too hard to get people

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.930
to sign up for SpaceX training sessions.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.070
Even if they have to sit in a centrifuge until
they throw up.

00:10:40.070 --> 00:10:42.459
The second issue with point-to-point is logistics.

00:10:42.459 --> 00:10:47.510
It’s not uncommon to spend all day traveling,
even when your flight is only a few hours

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:48.510
long.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:52.800
The ride to the airport, the traffic, the
transfers, and security all start to add up,

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.540
and pretty quickly you’ve spent 8 hours
traveling just to get from LA to San Francisco.

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:02.500
If SpaceX has to land their rockets hours
outside of major city centers, just getting

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:07.480
to and from the space port could eat away
at any time savings from the rocket ride.

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:11.639
This is a great point, but remember, we’re
thinking about this project in the context

00:11:11.639 --> 00:11:17.490
of decades, not years, so it’s not crazy
to imagine the necessary support infrastructure

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:21.100
to become a reality while SpaceX is working
on point-to-point.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:26.180
Things like autonomous drone ships, self-driving
cars, underground tunnels, and even hyperloop

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:30.881
trains could all start to become a reality
around the same time as point-to-point, which

00:11:30.881 --> 00:11:32.860
would make everything a lot faster.

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:37.870
The third problem everyone cites with point
to point is cost, and this is the most important

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.680
factor in understanding exactly how much money
SpaceX could make here.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:45.910
People say “Surely it can’t be economical
to get on a rocket everytime you want to go

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:48.320
somewhere” and that’s definitely true
right now.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:53.570
The average cost of a long-haul flight is
about $2,500 dollars and experts think that

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:58.589
SpaceX won’t be able to break even unless
they are charging at least $12 thousand dollars,

00:11:58.589 --> 00:11:59.589
per person.

00:11:59.589 --> 00:12:01.810
The key here comes back to market size again.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:07.650
There are already 150 million people who take
flights longer than 10 hours.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:10.750
These long-haul flights generate a lot of
money.

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:12.910
Over $400 billion dollars annually.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:17.899
If SpaceX can get just 5% of the market to
switch over to point-to-point, they will be

00:12:17.899 --> 00:12:20.550
generating $20 billion dollars per year.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:25.910
Airlines routinely sell more than 5% of customers
on first-class seats, and those don’t get

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:26.959
you there any faster.

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:31.760
A massive market like that is a huge incentive
to get this working and if it doesn’t break

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:34.889
the laws of physics, Elon will try and figure
it out.

00:12:34.889 --> 00:12:39.960
There is one other potential problem with
SpaceX’s point-to-point strategy and that’s

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.769
competition from faster commercial airlines.

00:12:42.769 --> 00:12:47.970
Since the concord was decommissioned in 2003,
traveling faster than the speed of sound has

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:50.440
been impossible for civilian travelers.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:55.019
But a startup named Boom has been developing
supersonic jets that can travel at Mach 2.2.

00:12:55.019 --> 00:12:59.509
This could make the high-speed travel market
much more competitive, but that’s a story

00:12:59.509 --> 00:13:00.579
for another video.

00:13:00.579 --> 00:13:01.449
Thanks for watching.

